from ./Selection import Selecting, Selection

export abstract anchor Expectation extends Selection as analysis-expectation:
    description:: string
    aspects:: string[]: []
    aspectsPerState:: string[]: []
    definedParts:: boolean: false

export anchor Expectations:
    description:
        Completeness only means something relative to a purpose. Nobody can
        write down every imaginable detail of a button, and a measure that
        asked for that would always read zero. So analysis asks a fixed set of
        questions instead, and completeness is the share of them the
        specification answers.

        An expectation is a set of such questions, aimed at some entities.
        There is one built-in expectation, and a project adds its own for the
        kinds of thing it specifies, because only the project knows what a
        complete description of one of its components looks like. Which
        entities an expectation applies to is chosen as described in
        @{Selecting}.

    questions:
        An expectation can ask three kinds of question.

        ```text
        definedParts      for every part, aspect or transformation the entity's
                          facts refer to: is it defined?
        aspects           for each listed aspect: does the entity have a fact
                          about it?
        aspectsPerState   for each value of each choice the entity has, and each
                          listed aspect: does a fact say what the aspect is in
                          that state?
        ```

        A question is answered by a fact that is structural, stated or
        derived, and is not partial, restricted or contested. A fact with no
        conditions answers a per-state question for every state it is not
        restricted from. A capability answers for its able form, so Button
        can be clicked answers a question about clickable. Two expectations
        that ask the same question each count it.

    builtIn:
        The built-in expectation, DefinedParts, applies to every entity and
        asks only that everything the entity's facts refer to is defined. On
        its own it measures whether a specification is closed: whether it
        uses anything it never explains.

    example:
        Applied to the Button, DefinedParts asks five questions and two are
        answered.

        ```text
        Is Button.action defined?   no     only used, in description/s2
        Is Button.color defined?    yes    Button.color = blue
        Is Button.text defined?     no     only used, in disabled/s2
        Is Button.state defined?    yes    Button.state is one of enabled, disabled
        Is dim defined?             no     only applied
        ```

        A project whose components live under spec/shape could add this.

        ```piton
        // analysis-expectation comes from the analysis module. It is declared here only so
        // that this example compiles on its own.
        abstract anchor Expectation as analysis-expectation:
            description:: string
            paths:: string[]: []
            aspects:: string[]: []
            aspectsPerState:: string[]: []

        analysis-expectation InteractiveComponent:
            description: Every component says how it looks and behaves in each of its states.
            paths:
                - spec/shape/**
            aspects:
                - state
                - action
            aspectsPerState:
                - color
                - clickable
        ```

        For a Button under spec/shape, that adds six questions, of which five
        are answered: state is, action is not, and color and clickable are
        answered in both states, by Button.color = blue and d1 when enabled
        and by the facts in the disabled property when disabled. Completeness
        becomes seven of eleven.

    expectations: {EXPECTATIONS}

export analysis-expectation DefinedParts:
    description: Everything an entity's facts refer to is defined.
    definedParts: true

export EXPECTATIONS:: extends Expectation[]:
    - {DefinedParts}
